Usage:
#build first
./build.sh 
#command line interaction 
./main 
#batch processing
cat input.txt | ./main


Design of Assignment

fields:
User (name)
Listing (Id,Title,Description,Price,Username,Creatime,Category)
Category (name,count)

implemention:
User hashtable [user]=1 
1,REGISTER O(1)

Listing hashtable [id]=item
1,CREATE_LISTING  O(1)
2,DELETE_LISTING  O(1)
3,GET_LISTING O(1) 

Category hashtable [catname][listid]=>item
	 hashtable [catname]=>count
         highest_name //string
         highest_count //int
	 zset //sortedset

1,GET_CATEGORY O(NlogN)
If it is a read heavy operation, There are two optimized solutions:
a,we can used a sortedset or balance tree(E.g:red black tree) to keep listings in order,so it doesnot need to sort every time,but it cost O(logN) to create_listing.
b,we can also used a hashtable to save it after getting,but it cost extra memory to store. 


2,GET_TOP_CATEGORY O(1)
a,if used sortedset , it cost O(logN) while create_listing & delete_listing to keep sortedset in order.
b,if no sortedset , it cost O(N) while delete_listing of highest category to regenerate highest category //Usually delete of listing is a low frequency event, this is the preferred solution.


